# @colis Package Feedback — Consuming Project Rule

## When to File an Issue

File a bug report when you observe that a `@colis/*` package:
- Behaves differently from its documented API (check `get_component_api`)
- Throws unexpected errors during normal usage
- Produces incorrect output, wrong styling, or broken theming

File a feature request when:
- A component is missing an essential prop or event the project needs
- A theme token doesn't exist but is clearly needed for the design
- A package lacks functionality that would broadly benefit consumers

## Before Filing — Check for Duplicates

**Always call `list_issues` before filing.** Provide the relevant package and type filter.
If an existing open issue covers the same problem, do not file a duplicate.

```
list_issues({ package: "@colis/keel", type: "bug" })
```

## How to File a Bug

Use the `report_issue` MCP tool. Include your expert persona details so the issue is
attributed correctly on GitHub.

Required fields:
- `package` — the full scoped name, e.g. `@colis/keel`
- `title` — short, descriptive
- `description` — what happened, steps to reproduce, expected vs. actual
- `expert_name` — your persona name from `.cursor/rules/experts/` (e.g. "Allison Foster")
- `expert_role` — your expertise area (e.g. "Accessibility Expert")

Optional but helpful:
- `project` — name of this consuming project
- `version` — package version where the bug was observed

## How to File a Feature Request

Use the `request_feature` MCP tool.

Required fields:
- `package` — e.g. `@colis/keel`
- `title` — short, descriptive
- `use_case` — why the feature is needed, what problem it solves
- `expert_name` and `expert_role` — your persona from `.cursor/rules/experts/`

Optional:
- `project` — consuming project that needs this
- `proposed_solution` — your implementation suggestion

## Closing an Issue (Confirming a Fix)

When the `@colis` packages team marks an issue `status:awaiting-confirmation`, they have
shipped a fix and are waiting for **you** (the opener) to confirm it works.

After upgrading the affected package and verifying the fix:

```
close_issue({
  issue_number: 42,
  comment: "Confirmed fixed in @colis/keel v0.3.0 — button contrast now passes WCAG AA."
})
```

**Only the consuming project that filed the issue should close it.**
Do not close issues filed by other projects or unrelated to the current bug.

## Important Rules

- **Never include commit messages that trigger GitHub auto-close** such as "fixes #42" or
  "closes #42". Always use `close_issue` via MCP instead.
- **Only the opener closes.** The `@colis` maintainers can mark `status:resolved` but cannot
  close an issue — that right belongs to the consuming project that opened it.
- Attribute every issue to your expert persona. Issues filed as "Cursor Agent" are harder to
  triage and may be deprioritised.
- If `gh` CLI is not installed or not authenticated, the tool will return instructions on how
  to set it up.

## Label Reference

| Label | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `status:open` | Filed, not yet acknowledged |
| `status:in-progress` | @colis team is actively working on it |
| `status:resolved` | Fix shipped — waiting for opener to confirm |
| `status:awaiting-confirmation` | Same as resolved — please close when confirmed |
| `source:ai-agent` | Automatically applied to all issues filed via MCP |
